          "type": "intro",
          "title": "The Search for Unshakeable Ground",
          "content": "Imagine every book you've ever trusted might be wrong. Every teacher, every sense impression, every \"obvious\" truth — all potentially false. That's the position **René Descartes** (1596-1650) put himself in on purpose.\n\nDescartes is often called \"the father of modern philosophy\" because he refused to inherit his beliefs from Aristotle, the Church, or common sense. Instead he asked a brutal question: if I doubt everything that COULD possibly be false, is there anything left standing?\n\nThis lesson walks through that experiment — the **Method of Doubt** — and the single truth Descartes claimed survived it: the **Cogito**, \"I think, therefore I am.\"",
